hi everyone i'm excited to be part of  this meeting thank you for the  organizers for putting everything  together especially  uh with everything going on right now um  my name is nicole and i'm a phd student  in the berkshire lab at the university  of rochester today i'm going to tell you  about a project that's actually been  rapidly evolving in the lab since  quarantine  due to it being mostly computationally  based and it involves a tool that we've  developed in order to analyze epithelial  architecture  so just to orient you to my talk  uh to begin with i've got to kind of  split this into three  sections first i'm gonna briefly go over  some background and uh labs  interests um second i'll go over the  tool and how it works  and then lastly we'll look at uh what  we've learned from the tools so far  so what are we interested in this lab  our lab is interested in how epithelial  tissues are built and maintained  um so as many of you probably know  epithelial tissues are the most common  tissue type in the body  they come in different flavors so to  speak so for example we have  simple columnar epithelia that line the  intestine  and we have simple squamous in the lung  but all epithelial tissues are sheets of  cells that line surfaces of the body and  provide protection from the outside  environment  and oftentimes the sheets of cells are  organized into tissues with specific  shapes and these shapes are essential  for  function so here are a few examples  coming back to the intestinal cells  uh these are organized into both crypts  in the bottom here and villi  and this serves two functions um first  of the crips which house the stem cells  down here  are protected from the lumen so um  that's obviously important so that the  stem cells can keep replenishing  the cells that are shed from the top of  the villi  and then the villi themselves protrude  into the lumen to increase the surface  area and maximize nutrient uptake  um second example here is the kidney  epithelia these are organized into  tubules and the shape of the tubule  uh and the organization vary depending  on where  in the chain of the kidney they are  um but they're all optimized for their  specific function  and last of the alveolar cells in the  lung  are simple squamous and this is  optimized for  gas exchange so in all of these examples  the organization of the epithelial cells  is actually essential for the organs to  function properly and  misplaced cells are thought to lead to  diseases such as  cancer so how do cells organize into  these shapes  um there are many sort of behaviors that  drive tissue shape change and  development but as a lab we're  particularly interested in  these bottom two here so cell division  and cell integration  um the cell division in particular poses  a problem for  a tissue in that the cell undergoing  mitosis is greatly changing shape as  it's growing  and rounding and then it has to undergo  cytokinesis and the two dwarfs have to  be placed within the tissue  or without compromising the integrity of  that tissue  and just to emphasize that point here  are two movies of  cells that are undergoing mitosis um the  first example here  is uh there's drosophila follicular  epithelium while  on the right is a mouse intestinal  organoid in both  movies the membranes are marked in green  and then tubulin is marked in red  and you can see that in both the cells  have rounded moved apically in the  tissue  and then the two daughters are born  slightly out of the plane of the tissue  here it's round towards the lumen and  then in both examples after division the  two daughters return  basically and reincorporate back into  the tissue  and all while the rest of the tissue  isn't affected  everything remains intact and  everything's still functioning well  so we are using mdck cells  um for monolayers to look at how  epithelial layers are built  so mdck cells are derived from adult  kidney and as you can see from this  fixed imaging and from the movie  which i'll play the sheets of the cells  undergo many divisions even when the  tissue already looks pretty packed so  this looks pretty regular but you can  see  here highlighted many many cell  divisions and this is all happening  without these other cells being  compromised  um just to look at the side view of  these um  when we grow mdck cells on a  collagen-coated slide they form  polarized monolayers in culture  so they're not inherently polarized but  we can induce polarity  and here you can see that there's a  nicely packed sheet of cells and if we  look at the xc view  we can see that abc is localized  apically  so a marker of uh polarity apical  polarity here  and um also then just to point out we  use  floyd into stain for actin and then  flappy for the dna as well  um so these you know monolayers look  quite good  and by looking at them we could tell  they're a layer um but  actually we see a lot of variation in  the organization  across the slide so we can see  areas that are very sparse and very flat  we see nice areas like the example i  just showed you  and we can see areas where it looks  quite disorganized  so this implies that the unit  development of the  layer isn't uniform across the uh  slide and so we need a way  to classify these layers that is in an  unbiased and consistent manner  and that's where our goal comes in so  this um  the whole point of this project is to be  able to characterize mdck monolayer  development  in an accurate and unbiased manner and  that brings me to part two of my talk  which is what is at all and how does it  work  so just to quickly go over what we  expect an  mtck monolayer to look like um i'll show  you a few examples but just to go into  in a bit more detail  when the layers formed we see this  nicely packed organized uh  cells and then if we look at the xz  view we can see that the nuclei are l  towards the bottom of the tissue  and the actin is enriched along the  lateral and apical signal  surfaces sorry um so we know this by  just looking at the layer but we need a  way to computationally import this  information  pixel by pixel and determine where the  layer is  in the image so to do that we  um created a pipeline for image  acquisition so  first off we plate ourselves on a  collagen coated slide as i've said  um and then after 24 hours we fix and  stain the layers  and then we image the same 16 positions  within the well so this as i said the  organization across the well doesn't um  appear to be uniform so we don't want to  be picking and choosing where we image  and then once we have these images um we  use the cells tool in a mars to segment  out all of the nuclei in the image  shown down here and this then gives us  the x y and z positions of all the  nuclei as well as the volumes  so then we can use this information as  well as the original  corresponding image file to load into  our automated layer analysis tool which  is alan  so the first step in here is to go  through the nuclei and just filter out  anything that  has been segmented that isn't a true  nucleus so anything that's probably  debris or anything that's just  crazy too big um so that we get all of  the  signal that seems to be a real nuclear  and then  um we run this along with the image file  through uh the rest of the script and  this determines where the layer is by  looking at both  the positional information of the nuclei  as well as the actin signal  so we go through um step by step in  z summing the x y uh actin signal and  this gives us the blue profile here  and then we can set the bottom and the  top cutoffs based on that actin signal  so i'm not going to go into any more  detail specifically for just for time  sake about how this works but if you  have any questions feel free to email me  um and we went through just to make sure  that this  seems to be working uh well and we a few  of us in the lab  took um some examples said where we  thought the top and the bottom of the  layers were  and uh compared that to allen and we  matched up pretty well  um one thing i just want to point out  here as well is that there is quite a  lot of variation  in manual uh determination of the top  and the bottom of the layer so this just  emphasizes the fact as to why we want to  actually do this computationally  but overall we matched up pretty well  with these  measurements that the tool is uh giving  us  so we can also tell from these images  and from loading everything through the  script whether or not our cells have  actually formed a layer  so we can go through and then based on  where the nuclei profile is  in a relationship to the actin profile  we can say whether or not the layer is  actually too flat so it's not actually a  proper layer it's probably a unpolarized  layer um  we can say whether it's too disorganized  so this one looks like the cells can  just kind of  clumped up on top of one another rather  than forming a nice uh  layer and you can see that the action  profile looks pretty crazy on this side  um and we can also just you know only  then focus on the ones that are just  right for the analysis that we wish to  do  um so just to point out here this works  really well on  examples that form either really flat  layers  very disorganized layers or are very  clearly a layer the borderline examples  are giving us a little bit of trouble  right now but we're optimizing that  so i just want to point that out and  then so for that reason the rest of the  presentation has also been manually  verified all of the data  so coming to that what is it that we've  learned from alan so far  so um one of the things that we actually  can get from alan is that we can  distinguish between developing  mature layers so we see that at eight  hours post-plating  the image is classed as a layer  according to alan  and all of the nuclei are towards the  bottom of the actin signal  and the um we get the nice uh  acting curve here um but the  as we go through time we start to see  this  little shoulder appearing in the actin  signal and a sharper peak for the apical  signal here  so um we can see that in the projection  as well that the  uh apical signal seems to becoming more  intense  as well as the lateral signal as the  layer becomes more organized  so as it becomes more regular we get to  see this specific  uh profile in the actin which is pretty  cool  um so another thing that we have found  from  alan is that between eight hours and 24  hours  when we pleat 200 000 cells we actually  see that  um at eight hours none of these images  are classed as a polarized layer  the majority of them are actually  unpolarized and flat  but over 24 hours we see a good  proportion of these actually becoming  polarized layers so  this is good that we can actually work  this out but we wanted to know  whether this polarization is actually  due more to  cell proliferation or reorganization of  cells over time so  in order to answer this question we need  to tease apart  either we need to tease apart time and  density and layer development  so to do this we can do various  different manipulations to ourselves so  we can change the initial plating number  either 100 000 versus 200 000 and we can  change the time  8 hours 24 hours or 48 hours and or we  can actually change  and stop cell division so we can use a  g1 inhibitor  um to stop the cells from dividing to  then  see just what contribution the  reorganization of cells can have over  time  so we did these three different  manipulations but for each one the data  i'm going to show you  is all the same final density so this  way we've decoupled  um density from time or cell number  initial cell number that is um and  division so  we're looking at all the same final  density but we've got there in different  ways  so when we look at that data we actually  see  that um if we treat the cells with  riscovitine which is that g1 inhibitor  that is um  saying about we see that actually  none of those over 24 hours form  layers so all of them are unpolarized or  disorganized  so but if we look at um  the same plating number initially and  the same time period  we see that actually that a lot of these  form uh nice polarized layers  so this way we've uncoupled time  cell number and just looking at cell  division here and it seems that  cell division might actually primarily  be driving the organization of  the layer and it's not really the  initial cell number  or time that matters um so that's kind  of cool  we want to look into this in a little  bit more detail and how we're going to  do this  is to build a computational model to  further tease apart the relationship  between division time  and layer development so by doing it  this way  we can manipulate individual variables  and look  and see what uh combination of variables  actually are giving us the layers that  we see  um so with that i'll just conclude so we  have developed a custom script that  determines whether or not  mdck cells have formed a nice smaller  layer um we can determine how many  nuclei  are within that layer and on top of them  on the layer too i didn't go into that  with so much detail but we're doing a  lot of work with that  um and we can look and determine the  stage of the layer development  so the number of extra layer nuclei also  increases resultant see i didn't show  you this  um but we are looking very much into  whether or not the  extra layered nuclei are there because  of  um cells just landing on top initially  and cells landing on top and then  dividing  um or cells leaving the layer or both so  we're looking at that  and we also have seen that organization  is actually independent  of cell density and time but definitely  requires subdivision  so moving on we want to use this  computation modeling to further  investigate this relationship  between cell division time and layer  formation  and we're currently making our script  more user-friendly and doing the  optimization that i mentioned at the  beginning  um so it can be more accessible to other  people to use  and we are starting to look at our labs  molecules of interest primarily  sardisian molecules and we can  manipulate these individually and then  see how they also affect  layer formation um so with that i'd like  to thank  my lab this is how we've all seen each  other recently during quarantine um  in particular i'd like to thank another  phd student in the lab christian who's  helped me a lot with the coding um i'm  not a computational biologist  so without him this would have been a  lot slower um  and with that if you have any questions  feel free to email me  thank you for listening

